WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.410 --> 00:00:04.430
Films were once seen as superior to television
in Hollywood, but with the rise of streaming

00:00:04.430 --> 00:00:09.170
platforms and cable, television is enjoying
a surge in popularity and prestige like never

00:00:09.170 --> 00:00:09.670
before.

00:00:10.060 --> 00:00:14.260
Now, even A-list talent is flocking to television
to tackle meaty roles once reserved for the

00:00:14.260 --> 00:00:14.800
big screen.

00:00:15.340 --> 00:00:19.840
As the New Golden Age of Television stretches
on, here are a few TV roles everyone in Hollywood

00:00:19.840 --> 00:00:20.500
wanted to play.

00:00:21.200 --> 00:00:22.720
Carrie Bradshaw, Sex and the City

00:00:23.300 --> 00:00:27.800
It was Sarah Jessica Parker's career-defining
role, but other actresses nearly donned sex

00:00:27.800 --> 00:00:32.280
columnist Carrie Bradshaw's little tutu on
the hit HBO comedy Sex and the City.

00:00:32.660 --> 00:00:36.420
Series creator Darren Star initially asked
China Beach and Desperate Housewives star

00:00:36.420 --> 00:00:41.740
Dana Delany to play Carrie after seeing her
onstage with Kim Catrall . Delany declined,

00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:46.000
not wanting to be in a show about sex — although
she encouraged Cattrall to accept the role

00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:46.660
of Samantha.

00:00:46.980 --> 00:00:51.660
Parker was next on Star's list, but she was
unsure, so he offered the role to Linda Edelstein

00:00:52.080 --> 00:00:54.920
- best recalled today for her role on House
- as a backup.

00:00:55.360 --> 00:01:00.140
Edelstein signed a contract, but Parker finally
said yes, leaving Star to let Edelstein go.

00:01:00.340 --> 00:01:00.940
Ouch.

00:01:01.400 --> 00:01:03.660
As Carrie Bradshaw knows, breaking up is hard
to do.

00:01:04.420 --> 00:01:12.220
"Why is it so hard… for you to factor me
into your life in any real way"

00:01:12.940 --> 00:01:14.180
Don Draper, Mad Men

00:01:14.680 --> 00:01:18.840
Don Draper is Sterling Cooper's best ad man,
capable of selling anything from the Kodak

00:01:18.840 --> 00:01:24.190
Carousel to Coca-Cola with equal parts showmanship
and charm — and future Emmy winner Jon Hamm

00:01:24.190 --> 00:01:28.680
needed to do some serious selling to convince
AMC and series creator Matthew Weiner he was

00:01:28.940 --> 00:01:33.220
the right man to fill Draper's immaculately
tailored 1960s suits in Mad Men.

00:01:33.680 --> 00:01:37.990
A virtual unknown at the time with a string
of tiny film and TV credits, Hamm was basically

00:01:37.990 --> 00:01:39.620
at the bottom of everyone's list.

00:01:39.890 --> 00:01:44.120
Hamm revealed to podcast host Marc Maron that
Mad Men's casting directors originally went

00:01:44.120 --> 00:01:47.960
to Punisher star Thomas Jane — who promptly
turned down their offer, saying he didn't

00:01:47.960 --> 00:01:48.580
"do television."

00:01:48.880 --> 00:01:53.600
Roger Sterling himself, actor John Slattery,
initially auditioned to play Don, but Weiner

00:01:53.600 --> 00:01:56.110
only wanted him for the wisecracking silver
fox Sterling.

00:01:56.380 --> 00:01:58.460
"They wanted me to read for is part, which
"who wouldn't?" you know…

00:01:58.460 --> 00:02:01.040
I thought I did a really good job actually."

00:02:01.740 --> 00:02:06.220
With both Jane and Slattery out of the running,
Weiner asked execs to look at two finalists:

00:02:06.360 --> 00:02:07.880
Hamm and Younger's Peter Hermann.

00:02:08.420 --> 00:02:12.520
AMC flew Hamm to New York, where he charmed
programming execs over drinks at the Gansevoort

00:02:12.520 --> 00:02:14.780
Hotel and won the role on the spot.

00:02:15.240 --> 00:02:16.700
Doing business during cocktails?

00:02:17.000 --> 00:02:18.340
Don Draper would surely approve.

00:02:19.020 --> 00:02:20.740
Sam and Diane, Cheers

00:02:21.160 --> 00:02:25.040
For the first half of Cheers' 11-season run,
the show's comedy was propelled by the sexual

00:02:25.040 --> 00:02:30.060
tension between egotistical, womanizing bar-owner
Sam Malone and brainiac feminist waitress

00:02:30.060 --> 00:02:31.100
Diane Chambers.

00:02:31.320 --> 00:02:36.320
Their will-they-won't-they love/hate relationship
became Must See TV and catapulted Ted Danson

00:02:36.320 --> 00:02:38.020
and Shelley Long to Emmy-winning fame.

00:02:38.500 --> 00:02:42.940
Coming off the success of Taxi, sibling writers
Glen and Les Charles and director James Burrows

00:02:43.090 --> 00:02:47.020
wanted to create another workplace comedy,
but set in a bar — and they knew Sam and

00:02:47.020 --> 00:02:48.700
Diane would be the key to making it work.

00:02:49.350 --> 00:02:53.100
They saw hundreds of actors before narrowing
it down to three pairs that were brought in

00:02:53.110 --> 00:02:57.670
for a final audition: Ted Danson and Shelley
Long, former football player Fred Dryer and

00:02:57.670 --> 00:03:01.700
Newhart's Julia Duffy, and Knot's Landing's
William Devane and Lisa Eichhorn.

00:03:02.060 --> 00:03:05.840
While the team really liked Dyer and Duffy,
the chemistry between Danson and Long was

00:03:05.840 --> 00:03:06.900
instant and undeniable.

00:03:07.560 --> 00:03:15.440
"This is it…we have sunk as low as human
beings can sink."

00:03:16.080 --> 00:03:17.520
Michael Scott, The Office

00:03:18.000 --> 00:03:22.420
Steve Carell became a star thanks to his brilliant,
cringe-worthy, and occasionally heartbreaking

00:03:22.420 --> 00:03:25.180
turn as Dunder Mifflin's bumbling boss, Michael
Scott.

00:03:25.600 --> 00:03:29.280
Showrunner Greg Daniels auditioned actors
a little differently for the show, conducting

00:03:29.290 --> 00:03:32.610
interviews and having them improv answers
as their characters.

00:03:32.610 --> 00:03:33.880
That probably made it pretty hard.

00:03:34.600 --> 00:03:37.280
"That's what she said…HAHAHAHA."

00:03:37.580 --> 00:03:41.600
Auditions for the show were a veritable who's-who
of comedy actors: Rainn Wilson was the first

00:03:41.620 --> 00:03:45.620
person to audition for Michael, but Daniels
felt he was far better suited for power-hungry

00:03:45.680 --> 00:03:46.420
Dwight Schrute.

00:03:46.820 --> 00:03:51.880
Hank Azaria [ from The Simpsons, Better Caul
Saul's Bob Odenkirk, and Alan Tudyk all auditioned

00:03:51.880 --> 00:03:56.700
for Michael as well, and NBC chief Kevin Reilly
offered the role to Oscar nominee Paul Giamatti,

00:03:56.880 --> 00:03:57.600
but he turned it down.

00:03:58.080 --> 00:04:02.060
Universal Pictures chairwoman Stacey Snider
encouraged the network to hire the ex-Daily

00:04:02.060 --> 00:04:05.400
Show correspondent Carell, and the rest was
sitcom history.

00:04:06.060 --> 00:04:07.620
Negan - The Walking Dead

00:04:08.080 --> 00:04:12.560
The Walking Dead fans spent months eagerly
anticipating the introduction of Negan, the

00:04:12.560 --> 00:04:15.380
show's most sadistic — and arguably most
compelling — villain.

00:04:15.640 --> 00:04:18.860
Of course, first the producers had to find
just the right person to play him.

00:04:19.400 --> 00:04:23.880
Punk rock icon and actor Henry Rollins told
Forbes he was the inspiration for the character's

00:04:23.880 --> 00:04:27.620
look in the original comic books, inspiring
Walking Dead artist Charlie Adlard.

00:04:28.300 --> 00:04:30.880
Rollins tried out for the role, for which
he thought he was a "shoo-in," but he didn't

00:04:30.880 --> 00:04:31.380
get it.

00:04:31.840 --> 00:04:36.220
Creator Robert Kirkman told MTV he thought
Jon Hamm of Mad Men "would be perfect" for

00:04:36.820 --> 00:04:38.120
Negan, and Hamm expressed interest.

00:04:38.440 --> 00:04:42.100
Garret Dillahunt, the versatile character
actor best known for playing the goofy grandpa

00:04:42.100 --> 00:04:46.470
on Raising Hope and two separate characters
on Deadwood, lobbied publicly on Twitter for

00:04:46.470 --> 00:04:46.970
the role.

00:04:47.340 --> 00:04:50.960
Evidently, none of them could swing it as
well as the guy who got the part: Jeffrey

00:04:50.960 --> 00:04:51.580
Dean Morgan.

00:04:52.380 --> 00:04:53.780
Scandal - Olivia Pope

00:04:54.380 --> 00:04:58.580
When word got out that Grey's Anatomy creator
Shonda Rhimes was working on a new show, lots

00:04:58.580 --> 00:04:59.680
of actresses were interested.

00:05:00.280 --> 00:05:04.980
Like Grey's Anatomy, that new series — titled
Scandal — offered prime roles for women,

00:05:04.980 --> 00:05:08.620
notably that of the lead character, Washington,
D.C. "fixer" Olivia Pope.

00:05:09.120 --> 00:05:13.540
Rhymes told The Hollywood Reporter that early
on, one ABC exec argued Pope "would be perfect

00:05:13.540 --> 00:05:17.280
for Connie Britton," to which she replied,
"It would be, except Olivia Pope is black."

00:05:17.700 --> 00:05:22.760
Rhymes had based the character on real-life
D.C. problem-solver Judy Smith, who is African-American,

00:05:23.060 --> 00:05:25.440
and she wanted to "honor her identity and
heritage."

00:05:25.760 --> 00:05:30.139
Gabrielle Union was a top contender, and told
Access Hollywood Live that she and "half of

00:05:30.139 --> 00:05:34.100
black Hollywood" were interested in the role,
adding that she made it into the final round

00:05:34.100 --> 00:05:35.380
of five or six actresses.

00:05:35.760 --> 00:05:40.360
Taraji P. Henson, who'd go on to play Cookie
Lyon on Empire, auditioned, but knew she wouldn't

00:05:40.360 --> 00:05:40.860
get it.

00:05:41.240 --> 00:05:45.340
"I auditioned for…Shonda Rhimes for Scandal.

00:05:46.080 --> 00:05:46.840
I didn't..

00:05:46.840 --> 00:05:47.640
Thank God Kerry got it."

00:05:47.780 --> 00:05:51.280
Indeed, actress Kerry Washington got the gig
— and she's perfect for it.

00:05:51.780 --> 00:05:52.760
Thanks for watching!

